module gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_cutover_cli

// THE ONE-TIME CUTOVER, IN ITS OWN MODULE (review 94530, and the parent's call on it).
//
// Everything below this header exists for a single run: the cutover that moves `retired` and `accepted` out of the
// authored carriers and into the roadmap event log (docs/runbooks/roadmap-event-log-cutover.md; operator ruling
// gunbc.design_ruling::plans_reset_and_audit_recommendations_2026_11_08). It began life at the foot of
// gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_event_cli, which is permanent: once the migration's verification receipt lands, the precheck
// can only answer "already migrated", the two gates only guard writes that no longer happen, and every declaration
// here is dead code sitting in the module that owns the permanent record routes. DESIGN's how_to_work puts the
// question to a carrier — will it survive the terminal architecture substantially unchanged, and be consumed by it?
// — and for this family the answer is no, so it is not left lying among the survivors. One module, one disposition,
// one deletion:
//
//     gunbc.witness.roadmap_cutover::cutover_module_scaffold   — the Scaffold
//     gunbc.witness.roadmap_cutover::cutover_module_deletions  — the file and declarations that go with it
//
// WHAT STAYS BEHIND, AND WHY THIS IS NOT A SPLIT OF ONE ROUTE IN TWO. The permanent entries this migration needs —
// `roadmap_event_record_cli`, `roadmap_acceptance_record_cli`, `roadmap_acceptance_line_of` — stay in
// gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_event_cli and are imported from there: they own the append, the operation identity and the
// `EventAlreadyPresent` refusal that makes a replayed write impossible rather than merely unusual. The declarations
// here only pre-gate those entries for the duration of the migration — read the log first through the production
// readers, and admit a write only for a node or receipt the model itself lists as part of this migration. Read that
// way the boundary is not arbitrary: the permanent authority and its refusal law on one side, the migration's
// admission on the other, and the second file is the one that gets deleted.
//
// Nothing outside this module imports it except the runbook's commands and dag/test/claim/roadmap/
// roadmap_cutover_witness.dag, which is the test of the claim above: if a permanent caller appears, the module is
// no longer one-time and this header is a lie that the claims in that file will show.

import std.types { String, Bool, List, NonEmptyStr }
import std.process { ProcessExit, ExitSuccess, ExitFailure }
import std.algebra { trim, string_contains, zip_eq }
import extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io { Filesystem }
import gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_model {
  RoadmapNodeId, RoadmapAcceptanceReceipt, roadmap_acceptance_line_acceptance_receipt, roadmap_node_ids_contain,
}
import gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_event_log {
  RoadmapRetiredNodeIds,
  roadmap_migration_retirement_ids, roadmap_migration_retirement_authority,
  roadmap_migration_acceptance_identities, roadmap_acceptance_migration_identity,
}
import gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_authority { roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load, roadmap_retired_node_ids_load }
import gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_acceptance_history_carrier { RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoad }
import gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_event_cli { roadmap_acceptance_line_of, roadmap_acceptance_record_cli, roadmap_event_record_cli }

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE PRE-WRITE REFUSAL FOR THE ONE-TIME CUTOVER
// (docs/runbooks/roadmap-event-log-cutover.md, step `precheck`; operator ruling
// gunbc.design_ruling::plans_reset_and_audit_recommendations_2026_11_08).
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. The cutover appends facts the log does not yet hold: five RetiredEvents and the
// seventeen operator acceptances plus its one revocation. Appending any of them a SECOND time is not
// harmless — a second RetiredEvent on a chain is a second terminal event the replay folds, and a
// duplicated acceptance is a receipt the audit trail shows twice. The append route beneath this one
// already refuses an event whose (node, id, parent, kind) is already present, but that refusal lands
// DURING the write, after earlier steps of the same attempt have already landed, which is the worst
// moment to discover the migration ran before. So the runbook asks this question BEFORE it writes
// anything, through the two readers production itself uses (roadmap_retired_node_ids_load and
// roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load — the same loads the board, the page and the belt read), and
// refuses the whole attempt if any fact it is about to add is already live.
//
// WHAT MAKES IT A MODEL AND NOT A GREP. Nothing here opens a file, lists a directory or matches a
// word in a document. Each reader's answer is matched by its OWN TYPE, and a reader that refuses is
// reported as a refusal — an unreadable log never answers "nothing is there yet"
// (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed). The runbook's shell runs this entry and aborts its own block
// on a nonzero exit; it decides nothing.
//
// WHY THE TWO ROSTERS ARE ARGUMENTS. `roadmap_cutover_precheck_decide` takes the retirement roster
// and the acceptance closed set as parameters and its only production caller passes the MODEL's own
// (`roadmap_migration_retirement_ids`, `roadmap_migration_acceptance_identities`), which is what the
// runbook and its emitted roster file are built from. They are parameters so a claim can drive the
// decision over a roster it constructs — a decision that could only be reached by calling the model
// could only be witnessed by a live carrier, and a witness that needs the host to be wrong is not a
// witness (gunbc.design_argument::claims_discriminate). Nothing outside this module passes anything
// else: a caller that invented a roster would be arguing with the model in its own refusal text.
//
// THE ROSTER FILE. The admitted decision also emits the roster of nodes step 4 must retire, one id
// per line, so the loop that retires them iterates WHAT THE MODEL LISTS rather than a copy of it
// typed into a document — a roster restated in shell is a second home for the same fact
// (gunbc.design_argument::planning_has_one_home), and a copy is how a later fifth or sixth id gets
// missed. Step 4 and step 6 read this file; nothing hard-codes the ids.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

type RoadmapCutoverPrecheck
  = PrecheckAdmitted { retired_live: List<String>, acceptances_live: List<String> }
  | PrecheckRefused { refusals: List<String> }

// THE VERDICT CROSSES TO THE WRITE DOORS AS A TYPED CARRIER, NOT AS A SENTENCE (review 94487). The
// precheck's answer is already a type, so what the doors consume is a machine line this module BOTH writes
// and parses: one pair of literals, in one function, compared by whole-line equality. DESIGN puts a status
// in a typed carrier (gunbc.design_argument::source_annotations) and is explicit that "a typecheck and a
// .contains() grep are not consumers" (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed) — the doors used to search the
// human report for the admitted sentence, so rewording that sentence would have refused every later write,
// with nothing tying the two string literals together. The sentence stays, for the operator and for the PR
// quote; it decides nothing.
type RoadmapCutoverReportVerdict
  = ReportVerdictAdmitted { token: String }
  | ReportVerdictRefused { token: String }
  | ReportVerdictUnreadable { detail: String }

// THE ONLY SOURCE OF EITHER LITERAL: the renderer writes what this returns and the parser below compares
// against what this returns, so a third spelling is not available.
fn roadmap_cutover_verdict_token(admitted: Bool) -> String {
  if admitted { "roadmap-cutover-precheck/v1 verdict=admitted" } else { "roadmap-cutover-precheck/v1 verdict=refused" }
}

// The verdict as it is written: the token ALONE on the first line, so nothing else on that line can be
// mistaken for it, and no prose further down can be mistaken for a verdict.
fn roadmap_cutover_precheck_verdict_line(decision: RoadmapCutoverPrecheck) -> String {
  let admitted = match decision {
    PrecheckAdmitted { retired_live: _, acceptances_live: _ } => true
    PrecheckRefused { refusals: _ } => false }
  join([roadmap_cutover_verdict_token(admitted: admitted), "\n"], "")
}

// WHAT THE DOORS READ, and why an answer that is not a verdict is a refusal. A report whose first line is
// neither token is not a precheck report — whatever it says further down — and "not a report" and "the report
// refused" both stop the write; the gate never treats an unreadable answer as an admitted one.
fn roadmap_cutover_precheck_report_verdict_parse(report_text: String?) -> RoadmapCutoverReportVerdict {
  match report_text {
    Absent => ReportVerdictUnreadable { detail: "the report file was not readable" }
    Present { value: text } => {
      let lines = split(s: text, delimiter: "\n")
      let head = match lines.first() {
        Present { value: l } => trim(s: l)
        Absent => "" }
      if head == roadmap_cutover_verdict_token(admitted: true) {
        ReportVerdictAdmitted { token: head }
      } else if head == roadmap_cutover_verdict_token(admitted: false) {
        ReportVerdictRefused { token: head }
      } else {
        ReportVerdictUnreadable { detail: join([
          "its first line is [", head,
          "], which is neither verdict token this module writes"], "") }
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE DECISION, PURE, so a claim can drive it without a host: the two readers' answers in, the
// roster out. `PrecheckAdmitted` carries what the log already holds about the migration's own facts,
// which is always empty for an admitted attempt — a non-empty live set is a refusal, never a note.
fn roadmap_cutover_precheck_decide(
  retired: RoadmapRetiredNodeIds,
  history: RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoad,
  retire_roster: List<String>,
  accept_roster: List<String>
) -> RoadmapCutoverPrecheck {
  match retired {
    RetiredNodeIdsRefused { detail } => PrecheckRefused { refusals: [
      join([
        "the live retired-node read REFUSED: ", detail as String,
        " — a log nobody can read is not a log that holds nothing; the migration would be appending blind"], "")] }
    RetiredNodeIdsLoaded { ids: live_retired } => match history {
      RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoadRefused { detail } => PrecheckRefused { refusals: [
        join([
          "the live acceptance-history read REFUSED: ", detail,
          " — an acceptance the migration cannot see is an acceptance it would record twice"], "")] }
      RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoaded { events: events } => {
        let live_acceptances = map(events, e => roadmap_acceptance_migration_identity(e: e))
        let already_retired = filter(
          retire_roster,
          n => roadmap_node_ids_contain(ids: live_retired, node: n as NonEmptyStr as RoadmapNodeId))
        let already_accepted = filter(
          accept_roster,
          i => count(filter(live_acceptances, a => a == i)) > 0)
        let empty_roster = if count(retire_roster) == 0 {
          ["the model hands back an EMPTY retirement roster — a migration that retires nothing silently skipped its step"]
        } else { [] }
        let empty_closed_set = if count(accept_roster) == 0 {
          ["the model hands back an EMPTY acceptance closed set — the no-pull-request door would admit nothing and the migration would record nothing"]
        } else { [] }
        // concat in this tree appends TWO lists (the precedent is gunbc/auth/profile_projection.dag:60, which
        // nests it); it does not flatten a list of lists, so four lists are joined by three nested calls.
        let refusals = concat(
          concat(
            concat(empty_roster, empty_closed_set),
            map(already_retired, n => join([
              "retirement is ALREADY LIVE for ", n,
              " (authority: ", match roadmap_migration_retirement_authority(node: n) {
                Present { value: a } => a as String
                Absent => "the roster carries no authority for this node" },
              ") — this migration is one-time; re-running it would append the fact twice"], ""))),
          map(already_accepted, i => join([
            "an acceptance is ALREADY LIVE for ", i,
            " — this migration is one-time; re-running it would record the receipt twice"], "")))
        if count(refusals) == 0 {
          PrecheckAdmitted { retired_live: already_retired, acceptances_live: already_accepted }
        } else {
          PrecheckRefused { refusals: refusals }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE DOCUMENT THE PR QUOTES. Rendered from the decision, with the two rosters read from the MODEL
// here rather than taken from the caller: this text is the artifact the runbook tells the operator to
// attach to the PR, and a renderer whose roster came from its caller could be made to quote any
// roster the caller liked. The machine verdict on the FIRST LINE is roadmap_cutover_precheck_verdict_line
// applied to the decision's TYPE, and the write doors parse that line and nothing else; rewording any
// human sentence here cannot change which side of the door an attempt lands on.
fn roadmap_cutover_precheck_document(decision: RoadmapCutoverPrecheck) -> String {
  let retire_roster = roadmap_migration_retirement_ids()
  let accept_count = count(roadmap_migration_acceptance_identities())
  let verdict = match decision {
    PrecheckAdmitted { retired_live: _, acceptances_live: _ } => "ADMITTED — none of the migration's own facts is live yet; the appends may run\n"
    PrecheckRefused { refusals: refusals } => join([
      "REFUSED — ", to_string(value: count(refusals)), " fact(s) of this migration are already live, or the log could not be read:\n",
      join(map(refusals, r => join(["  - ", r, "\n"], "")), ""),
      "Nothing was written by this refusal. The migration is one-time; if these facts are live, the cutover has already run.\n"], "")
  }
  let roster_text = match decision {
    PrecheckAdmitted { retired_live: _, acceptances_live: _ } => join([
      "RETIREMENT ROSTER, as the model lists it (", to_string(value: count(retire_roster)),
      " node(s); this is what the retirement step iterates — no id is typed into the runbook):\n",
      join(map(retire_roster, n => join(["  - ", n, "\n"], "")), "")], "")
    PrecheckRefused { refusals: _ } => ""
  }
  join([
    roadmap_cutover_precheck_verdict_line(decision: decision),
    "ROADMAP EVENT-LOG CUTOVER — PRE-WRITE PRECHECK\n",
    "(read-only: the two readers production uses, matched by their own types; no file of the carrier was opened by hand)\n",
    verdict, "\n", roster_text,
    "ACCEPTANCE CLOSED SET: ", to_string(value: accept_count),
    " identity(ies) pinned in gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_event_log roadmap_migration_acceptance_identities;\n",
    "every acceptance line the migration feeds must be one of them, or the modeled law refuses it.\n"], "")
}

// THE ENTRY THE RUNBOOK RUNS BEFORE ITS FIRST WRITE. Read-only against the carrier; the only files it
// touches are its own report and the roster file it emits for the retirement step. A refusal exits
// nonzero WITHOUT writing either, so a second run cannot leave behind artifacts that look like an
// admitted attempt.
fn roadmap_cutover_precheck_cli(report_path: String, roster_path: String) -> ProcessExit {
  let retire_roster = roadmap_migration_retirement_ids()
  let accept_roster = roadmap_migration_acceptance_identities()
  let decision = roadmap_cutover_precheck_decide(
    retired: roadmap_retired_node_ids_load(),
    history: roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load(),
    retire_roster: retire_roster,
    accept_roster: accept_roster)
  match decision {
    PrecheckRefused { refusals: refusals } => ExitFailure {
      code: 1,
      reason: join([
        "roadmap_cutover_precheck_cli: the cutover REFUSES to write — ", to_string(value: count(refusals)),
        " fact(s) of this one-time migration are already live or the log could not be read:",
        join(map(refusals, r => join(["\n  ", r], "")), ""),
        "; nothing was written to ", report_path, " or ", roster_path], "") }
    PrecheckAdmitted { retired_live: _, acceptances_live: _ } => {
      let document = roadmap_cutover_precheck_document(decision: decision)
      let roster_wrote = Filesystem.Write(
        path: roster_path,
        content: join(map(retire_roster, n => join([n, "\n"], "")), ""))
      let wrote = Filesystem.Write(path: report_path, content: document)
      if !roster_wrote.success {
        ExitFailure {
          code: 1,
          reason: join([
            "roadmap_cutover_precheck_cli: the retirement roster could not be written to ", roster_path,
            " (write error: ", roster_wrote.error,
            "); the retirement step iterates that file, so without it there is no step to run"], "") }
      } else if !wrote.success {
        ExitFailure {
          code: 1,
          reason: join([
            "roadmap_cutover_precheck_cli: the precheck report could not be written to ", report_path,
            " (write error: ", wrote.error,
            "); a cutover with no quotable pre-write read is a cutover nobody can audit"], "") }
      } else {
        ExitSuccess
      }
    }
  }
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE ORDERING GATE: no migration write without the precheck's admitted answer.
//
// WHY THIS IS A ROUTE AND NOT A LINE IN THE BOOK. Steps 0 and 4 sit in the same shell block, and the shell checked
// the ordering with `[ -s "$D/cutover.retire.roster" ]`. A guard in prose is silent when it breaks: the two
// statements stay adjacent, the file goes missing, and the append still reaches the carrier. The two witnesses that
// used to prove that guard held were cut with the rest of the runbook's shell (the note at the top of
// gunbc.witness.roadmap_cutover says why), so the guarantee moved HERE, where a claim can drive it and where an
// operator cannot walk past it by editing a paragraph: the migration entries below refuse a write unless the
// precheck's own report is readable, carries its admitted verdict, and — for a retirement — unless the roster on
// disk is exactly the roster the model hands step 0 and names the node being retired.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM. A report is a file, and a determined operator can type one. The gate makes an un-checked
// migration write impossible by accident, which is what a one-time migration needs; it is not a defence against a
// hand-written artifact, and it does not have to be — the log itself still refuses the duplicate (EventAlreadyPresent
// at the chain, the operation identity at the record), so a forged report produces a refusal downstream rather than a
// second copy of a fact.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
type RoadmapCutoverMigrationGate
  = GateAdmitted { fact: String }
  | GateRefused { detail: String }

// THE DOOR'S VERDICT TEST, in the reader's terms rather than the writer's prose (review 94487): the report's
// first line is compared, whole and trimmed, against the token roadmap_cutover_verdict_token produces — the same
// call the renderer used to write it. Searching the document for the admitted SENTENCE did fail closed, but it
// made a status live in prose: any line carrying that sentence admitted the write, the doors were tied to a
// human sentence with no reason to stay word-for-word stable, and rewording the renderer would have refused every
// later write with nothing to tie the two sides together. Three answers now, and only one opens the door.
fn roadmap_cutover_report_says_admitted(report_text: String?, fact: String) -> RoadmapCutoverMigrationGate {
  match roadmap_cutover_precheck_report_verdict_parse(report_text: report_text) {
    ReportVerdictAdmitted { token: _ } => GateAdmitted { fact: fact }
    ReportVerdictRefused { token: token } => GateRefused { detail: join([
      fact, " is refused: the precheck report in hand carries the REFUSED verdict (", token,
      ") — the last thing the log said about this migration was a refusal, and nothing may be appended",
      " behind it"], "") }
    ReportVerdictUnreadable { detail: detail } => GateRefused { detail: join([
      "no pre-write precheck report is readable for ", fact, " (", detail,
      ") — this migration is one-time and refuses to write un-checked; run the precheck step and re-run this",
      " one. A report typed by hand is not a substitute for the one the precheck writes"], "") }
  }
}

// Two lists agree when they are the same LENGTH and the same LINES IN THE SAME ORDER. This used to be
// `count(left) == count(right)` plus a nested membership fold, which was quadratic and wrong in the same
// breath: it accepted ["a","a"] against ["a","b"] — equal counts, the same two names, a duplicate on one
// side and a missing node on the other — and it accepted a reorder, while the book retires in roster order
// and the report is read line for line. Review 94530's second finding; sorting both sides would have kept
// the cost linear and kept that answer, because order is not irrelevant here. `zip_eq` is positional and
// fails when either list is left holding an item, so one pass covers length and order both.
// Discriminated by test.claim.roadmap.roadmap_cutover_witness::roster_agreement_is_line_for_line.
fn roadmap_cutover_string_lists_agree(left: List<String>, right: List<String>) -> Bool {
  zip_eq(a: left, b: right, eq: fn(l, r) { l == r })
}

fn roadmap_cutover_roster_lines_are_a_roster(lines: List<String>) -> Bool {
  count(lines) > 0
    && count(filter(lines, l => trim(s: l) == "" || string_contains(s: l, pattern: " "))) == 0
}

// A RETIREMENT is admitted only against the roster the model handed step 0. Roster absent, roster not the model's,
// and node not named by the roster are three separate refusals with three different remedies, because the operator
// standing in front of a refused step needs to know which file to go and look at.
fn roadmap_cutover_migration_gate_decide_retirement(
  node: String,
  report_text: String?,
  roster_lines: List<String>?,
  model_roster: List<String>
) -> RoadmapCutoverMigrationGate {
  let fact = join(["the retirement of ", node], "")
  match roadmap_cutover_report_says_admitted(report_text: report_text, fact: fact) {
    GateRefused { detail } => GateRefused { detail: detail }
    GateAdmitted { fact: _ } => match roster_lines {
      Absent => GateRefused { detail: join([
        fact, " is refused: the roster file step 0 emitted is not readable — the retirement step iterates that file,",
        " and a retirement that iterates nothing else is a second home for a fact the model already owns"], "") }
      Present { value: lines } =>
        if !roadmap_cutover_roster_lines_are_a_roster(lines: lines) {
          GateRefused { detail: join([
            fact, " is refused: the roster in hand is not a roster — blank or multi-token lines are not node ids",
            " (line-by-line, one id per line is what step 0 writes)"], "") }
        } else if !roadmap_cutover_string_lists_agree(left: lines, right: model_roster) {
          GateRefused { detail: join([
            fact, " is refused: the roster in hand is not the roster the model holds — it lists ",
            to_string(value: count(lines)), " node(s) against the model's ",
            to_string(value: count(model_roster)),
            "; a retirement step that iterates a list of its own is exactly the second home this cutover exists to remove"], "") }
        } else if count(filter(lines, n => n == node)) == 0 {
          GateRefused { detail: join([
            fact, " is refused: the roster the precheck admitted does not name it — step 4 retires the roster and",
            " nothing outside it"], "") }
        } else {
          GateAdmitted { fact: fact }
        }
    }
  }
}

// AN ACCEPTANCE is admitted only against the closed set the acceptance law itself enforces. Same report requirement;
// no roster file, because the precheck emits one roster (the retirement nodes) and the acceptances are pinned in the
// model, not on disk.
fn roadmap_cutover_migration_gate_decide_acceptance(
  identity: String,
  report_text: String?,
  closed_set: List<String>
) -> RoadmapCutoverMigrationGate {
  let fact = join(["the acceptance ", identity], "")
  match roadmap_cutover_report_says_admitted(report_text: report_text, fact: fact) {
    GateRefused { detail } => GateRefused { detail: detail }
    GateAdmitted { fact: _ } =>
      if count(closed_set) == 0 {
        GateRefused { detail: join([
          fact, " is refused: the model hands back an EMPTY acceptance closed set, so nothing can be admitted",
          " without pull-request evidence and a migration that records nothing is not a migration"], "") }
      } else if count(filter(closed_set, i => i == identity)) == 0 {
        GateRefused { detail: join([
          fact, " is refused: it is not one of the ", to_string(value: count(closed_set)),
          " identities pinned in gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_event_log roadmap_migration_acceptance_identities —",
          " the closed set is the only door for a receipt without a pull request, and it is also the door for this migration"], "") }
      } else {
        GateAdmitted { fact: fact }
      }
  }
}

// THE MIGRATION'S RETIREMENT ENTRY. Identical to roadmap_event_record_cli with kind=retire, except that nothing
// reaches the carrier until the gate above has admitted the node — so the book cannot retire by forgetting to check,
// and no edit to the book's guards can put the check back in front of the write.
fn roadmap_cutover_retire_cli(
  repo_root: String,
  node: String,
  text: String,
  author: String,
  report_path: String,
  roster_path: String
) -> ProcessExit {
  let report = Filesystem.Read(path: report_path)
  let roster = Filesystem.Read(path: roster_path)
  let gate = roadmap_cutover_migration_gate_decide_retirement(
    node: node,
    report_text: if report.success { Present { value: report.content } } else { Absent },
    roster_lines: if roster.success {
      Present { value: filter(split(s: roster.content, delimiter: "\n"), l => trim(s: l) != "") }
    } else { Absent },
    model_roster: roadmap_migration_retirement_ids())
  match gate {
    GateRefused { detail } => ExitFailure {
      code: 1,
      reason: join(["roadmap_cutover_retire_cli: ", detail,
        "; the retirement was NOT handed to the carrier (report ", report_path, ", roster ", roster_path, ")"], "") }
    GateAdmitted { fact: _ } => roadmap_event_record_cli(
      repo_root: repo_root, node: node, kind: "retire", text: text, author: author)
  }
}

// THE MIGRATION'S ACCEPTANCE ENTRY, same shape. A line this route cannot parse is handed to
// roadmap_acceptance_record_cli untouched, so the operator reads the located refusal the record route already knows
// how to give — the gate does not get to invent a worse message for a malformed line, and a malformed line never
// reaches the carrier either way.
fn roadmap_cutover_acceptance_record_cli(
  repo_root: String,
  receipt_line: String,
  pr_url: String,
  merged_at: String,
  head_sha: String,
  author: String,
  report_path: String
) -> ProcessExit {
  let report = Filesystem.Read(path: report_path)
  match roadmap_acceptance_line_of(text: receipt_line) {
    Absent => roadmap_acceptance_record_cli(
      repo_root: repo_root, receipt_line: receipt_line, pr_url: pr_url,
      merged_at: merged_at, head_sha: head_sha, author: author)
    Present { value: line } => match roadmap_acceptance_line_acceptance_receipt(line: line) {
      Absent => roadmap_acceptance_record_cli(
        repo_root: repo_root, receipt_line: receipt_line, pr_url: pr_url,
        merged_at: merged_at, head_sha: head_sha, author: author)
      Present { value: _ } => {
        let gate = roadmap_cutover_migration_gate_decide_acceptance(
          identity: roadmap_acceptance_migration_identity(e: line),
          report_text: if report.success { Present { value: report.content } } else { Absent },
          closed_set: roadmap_migration_acceptance_identities())
        match gate {
          GateRefused { detail } => ExitFailure {
            code: 1,
            reason: join(["roadmap_cutover_acceptance_record_cli: ", detail,
              "; the acceptance was NOT handed to the carrier (report ", report_path, ")"], "") }
          GateAdmitted { fact: _ } => roadmap_acceptance_record_cli(
            repo_root: repo_root, receipt_line: receipt_line, pr_url: pr_url,
            merged_at: merged_at, head_sha: head_sha, author: author)
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
